{"base":"main","body":"\u003c!-- entire-trail-link-start --\u003e\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/118\n\u003c!-- entire-trail-link-end --\u003e\n\nThis draft pull request was opened by [Entire](https://entire.io) after CI was requested for the linked trail. Feel free to edit the title or body — the link above is what keeps the trail and PR connected.\n\n\u003c!-- entire-shadow-pr --\u003e\n\n\u003c!-- CURSOR_SUMMARY --\u003e\n---\n\n\u003e [!NOTE]\n\u003e **Medium Risk**\n\u003e Changes how Windows test coverage is enforced in CI; regressions could slip through if sharding or verification has gaps, though the harness is heavily tested and fail-closed.\n\u003e \n\u003e **Overview**\n\u003e **Replaces** the single `windows-latest` entry in the main `test` matrix with a multi-job Windows race pipeline, while keeping the aggregate required check name **`test (windows-latest)`**.\n\u003e \n\u003e On Windows, CI now **compiles** the heavy `internal/cli` package once (`go test -race -c`), **inventories** runnable Test/Example/Fuzz roots, **plans** eight shards, runs them in parallel (up to 8 runners), runs remaining packages and **`go vet -race`** separately, then **verifies** on Linux that every planned root ran exactly once with matching binaries, SHAs, and race evidence. The workflow also sets **`permissions: contents: read`**.\n\u003e \n\u003e Adds **`tools/ci-bench/windows-production/`** (Python prepare/shard/other/verify scripts, planner settings, `inventory_testmain.go`, unit tests, README, and VM validation evidence). Linux/macOS race tests and the cross-build matrix are **unchanged**.\n\u003e \n\u003e \u003csup\u003eReviewed by [Cursor Bugbot](https://cursor.com/bugbot) for commit b90b5e3bab1051ceb8f38c46ba0e4b550ea5e0c3. Configure [here](https://www.cursor.com/dashboard/bugbot).\u003c/sup\u003e\n\u003c!-- /CURSOR_SUMMARY --\u003e","created_at":"2026-09-06T13:03:13Z","draft":true,"head":"codex/windows-ci-shards","head_repo":"entireio/entire-brain","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/pull/197","number":197,"sha":"b90b5e3bab1051ceb8f38c46ba0e4b550ea5e0c3","title":"Shard Windows race tests with exact coverage verification","updated_at":"2026-09-06T13:03:30Z"}
{"base":"main","body":"\u003c!-- entire-trail-link-start --\u003e\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/116\n\u003c!-- entire-trail-link-end --\u003e\n\nMoves the development and CI toolchain from Go 1.26.2 to Go 1.27.1.\n\n## Changes\n\n| File | Before | After |\n|---|---|---|\n| `go.mod` | `go 1.26` / `toolchain go1.26.2` | `go 1.27` / `toolchain go1.27.1` |\n| `mise.toml` | `go = { version = \"1.26.2\" }` | `go = { version = \"1.27.1\" }` |\n| `.github/workflows/test.yml` (5 pins) | `go-version: \"1.26.2\"` | `go-version: \"1.27.1\"` |\n| `README.md` | \"Go 1.26 toolchain for `entire-brain`\" | \"Go 1.27 toolchain for `entire-brain`\" |\n| `docs/getting-started.md` | \"A Go toolchain (1.26 or newer)\" | \"A Go toolchain (1.27 or newer)\" |\n\n`mise.toml` keeps the exact-patch pin the comment above it asks for (\"Keep aligned with the `toolchain` directive in go.mod\").\n\n### Deliberately left alone\n\nEverything else matching `1.26` lives under `benchmarks/agent-brain/` — `MULTI-REPOSITORY-NEGATIVE-CONTROL-RUN-PLAN-V2.md`, `TASK-POPULATION-CONTRACT.md`, and the `development-task-eligibility-*.json` / `development-task-negative-control-*.json` receipts. Those record the Go version each measurement actually ran under. Rewriting them would falsify the evidence, so they stay. A handful of test fixtures also use the literal string \"Uses Go 1.26\" as fact text (`internal/cli/facts_test.go`, `internal/factmerge/factmerge_test.go`, `internal/cli/reconcile_test.go`); it is arbitrary sample content, not a version requirement.\n\n## Verification (go1.27.1 darwin/arm64)\n\nRan the repo's own `check` gate lane by lane:\n\n| Lane | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `lint:go` (`go vet ./...`) | pass, no findings |\n| `lint:gofmt` (`gofmt -l -s .`) | clean — 0 files under both 1.26.5 and 1.27.1 |\n| `lint:gomod` (`go mod tidy`) | no-op beyond the directive change; `go.sum` unchanged |\n| `lint:shellcheck` | pass |\n| `go test ./... -count=1` | pass, 10 test packages (12 total), 0 failures |\n| `test:ci` (`go test -race ./...`) | pass, 0 data races |\n| `test:cgo` (`go test -race -tags \"brain_cgo sqlite_fts5\" ./...`) | pass, 0 data races |\n| `test:phase1` | pass, `internal/cli` 64.7s |\n| `build-all` | all five targets cross-compile (darwin/amd64, darwin/arm64, linux/amd64, linux/arm64, windows/amd64) |\n\nThe cgo path was the main risk and it is clean: `build:cgo` links a `go1.27.1` binary with `CGO_ENABLED=1` and `-tags=brain_cgo,sqlite_fts5`, the mattn + sqlite-vec vec0 + FTS5 stack compiles with no new warnings, and the resulting binary runs. Slowest package under `-race`: `internal/cli` at 220.7s pure-Go and 187.9s with the cgo tags.\n\nNothing regressed on 1.27. Unlike `entire-graph`, this repo needed no gofmt reformat — Go 1.27's alignment change happens not to touch any file here.","created_at":"2026-09-06T00:17:18Z","draft":false,"head":"chore/go-1.27","head_repo":"entireio/entire-brain","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/pull/195","number":195,"sha":"958ef458b422563ecfde81df8a95ff1ce64071ad","title":"chore(go): upgrade toolchain to Go 1.27.1","updated_at":"2026-09-06T00:17:22Z"}
{"base":"audit/ci-runs-benchmark-tests","body":"\u003c!-- entire-trail-link-start --\u003e\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/114\n\u003c!-- entire-trail-link-end --\u003e\n\nRunning the benchmark suite by discovery (#181) executed\n`benchmarks/agent-brain/confirmatory/test_task_negative_control.py` for the\nfirst time. It came back `FAILED (failures=1, errors=1)`. Both are pre-existing\ndefects in code #181 only exposed; neither is caused by #181. This PR fixes\nthem. Base is `audit/ci-runs-benchmark-tests`, so #181 goes green on its own\nterms.\n\n## 1. failure — the hanging-command test was timing the Go build\n\n`test_hanging_go_test_is_killed_by_the_only_timeout` asserted that\n`go test -count=1 -timeout=0 .` on a fixture whose body was `select {}` is still\nrunning after a 0.2s external timeout, and is therefore killed by it.\n\n**The fixture does not hang.** With every goroutine blocked and no timer\npending, the Go runtime's deadlock detector aborts the binary:\n\n```\nfatal error: all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!\nFAIL\texample.invalid/hang\t0.006s\n```\n\nThat is true on Linux and on macOS. What the assertion actually measured was the\nGo build in front of it: on a cold build cache the compile alone outlasts 0.2s\nand the test \"passes\"; on a warm one the whole invocation lands in ~0.08s and it\nfails. It passed on developers' Macs because `go test` there takes ~0.26s warm,\njust over the window. The property in the test's name — that a command which\nnever finishes is killed by the single external timeout — was never exercised:\nnothing was ever killed, because the process had already exited by itself.\n\nReproduced by running the module in a Linux container matching the CI job\n(ubuntu, Python 3.14, Go 1.26.2 + the primed `go1.26.4` pin): `'completed' !=\n'timeout'`, identical to CI.\n\n**Fix, preserving the intent rather than relaxing it:**\n\n- the fixture sleeps instead of `select {}`, which leaves a timer pending, so\n  the runtime keeps waiting and only the external timeout can end the run;\n- that package and a control package are compiled with `go test -c` *before*\n  anything is timed, so the build is outside the measured window;\n- the window is calibrated from a timed, fully cached run of the control\n  package instead of a hardcoded 0.2s, so a slow machine cannot decide the\n  result;\n- the test additionally asserts the whole window elapsed, so it cannot pass by\n  outlasting a fast exit. And since `_execution` returns only once the process\n  group is dead, a group kill that missed the test binary would now hang rather\n  than pass — under the old fixture nothing was alive to kill.\n\nVerified by mutation: put the `select {}` body back and the test fails.\n\n## 2. error — a Go telemetry sidecar was writing into the isolated state root\n\n`test_pinned_toolchain_module_preflight_fails_closed` errored in\n`TemporaryDirectory.__exit__`:\n\n```\nOSError: [Errno 39] Directory not empty: '/tmp/tmpXXXX/state'\n```\n\n`_reset_execution_state` builds a hermetic state root and `_test_environment`\npoints HOME, TMPDIR and the XDG directories at it. Go's telemetry directory is\n`os.UserConfigDir()/go/telemetry`, which therefore resolves *inside* that state\nroot — and at its default mode (`local`) every `go` invocation opens a counter\nfile there and fork+execs an upload sidecar that x/telemetry's own comment says\n\"may outlive the parent\".\n\nInstrumenting the state root shows it directly: at the moment `go` is reaped\nthere is no `telemetry/upload/`; two seconds later there is. So the state root\nkeeps being written to after the measured command has exited, and it races\nwhoever deletes it next.\n\nThat is unmeasured background work inside a run whose entire point is to be\nhermetic — the same class as the network access `GOPROXY=off`, `GOVCS=*:off` and\nthe blanked proxy variables already deny.\n\n- Linux: 3 cleanup failures in 40 iterations of that test body, the error naming\n  `state/xdg-config/go/telemetry`.\n- macOS: the same error at `state/home/Library/Application Support/go/telemetry`\n  — the same defect at the platform's own config directory, which is why the fix\n  writes the mode file to every path the toolchain may resolve\n  (`XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, `$HOME/.config`, `$HOME/Library/Application Support`)\n  rather than assuming one.\n\n**Fix:** `_reset_execution_state` writes a telemetry mode file of `off`. That is\nthe one mode in which no counter file is opened and no sidecar is started\n(x/telemetry `start.go` returns early). Not `ignore_cleanup_errors` — the leak\nis real and is now gone, not hidden. Same loop after the change: **0/60** on\nLinux, **0/80** on macOS.\n\nNo environment variable is added, so the receipt's `environment_manifest_sha256`,\n`go_env_sha256` and `execution_overrides` are unchanged.\n\n## Diagnosability\n\nThe bare status comparison is why this CI failure was opaque: `_execution`\nrecords only a digest of the command output, so `'completed' != 'timeout'` was\nthe entire report. `_execution` now takes an optional `output_sink` — never\nsupplied by receipt production, so no command output can reach a receipt through\nit — and the assertion message carries the window, the calibration, the exit\ncode, the elapsed time and the decoded output:\n\n```\n`go test ./hang` should have been killed by the only timeout (0.500s, calibrated\nfrom a 0.083s cached control run) but returned status='completed' exit_code=1\nafter 0.091s. Command output:\nfatal error: all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!\n```\n\nA new test, `test_isolated_execution_state_disables_the_go_telemetry_sidecar`,\npins the telemetry behaviour so it cannot regress silently.\n\n## Verification\n\n| where | result |\n|---|---|\n| Linux arm64, cold `GOCACHE`, fresh container per run | module 12/12 tests, **9/9 runs** |\n| Linux arm64, one container, cache warming across runs (the condition that broke it) | **12/12 runs** |\n| Linux **amd64** (the CI arch, emulated and ~10x slower — a stress test of the calibrated window) | **7/7 runs**, cold and warm |\n| macOS arm64 | module **18/18 runs**; full `ci_tests.py` green |\n| ENOTEMPTY loop, 60 iterations Linux / 80 macOS | **0 failures** (was 3/40 on Linux) |\n| full `benchmarks/agent-brain/ci_tests.py`, Linux, before vs after | the only difference is that `confirmatory/test_task_negative_control.py` moves from FAIL to ok; the other modules failing in that container are unchanged by this PR |\n","created_at":"2026-09-03T21:20:04Z","draft":false,"head":"fix/negative-control-timeout","head_repo":"entireio/entire-brain","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/pull/193","number":193,"sha":"481a171ca0b97e69ba5baa3536b6c35efa356c62","title":"fix(negative-control): two pre-existing defects the discovered benchmark suite exposed","updated_at":"2026-09-03T21:20:11Z"}
{"base":"main","body":"\u003c!-- entire-trail-link-start --\u003e\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/111\n\u003c!-- entire-trail-link-end --\u003e\n\n## The bug\n\n`semanticEndpointPath` derived a file path from a semantic relation endpoint ID with `strings.Split(id, \":\")` and returned `parts[2]`. That path is what `.brainignore` is matched against.\n\nentire-graph builds endpoint IDs by joining **unescaped** fields with `\":\"`:\n\n```\nsymbol:   \u003crepo-key\u003e:\u003clanguage\u003e:\u003cpath\u003e:\u003ckind\u003e:\u003cqualified-name\u003e\nfile:     \u003crepo-key\u003e:file:\u003cpath\u003e\nexternal: external:\u003ckind\u003e:\u003cvalue\u003e\n```\n\nThree of those fields may legitimately contain `\":\"` — a file path (`od:d/mod.py`), a local repo key (`local/\u003cdirectory name\u003e`), and a qualified name (an Express route symbol is named `/users/:id`). So the fixed-index read landed on the wrong segment:\n\n| case | endpoint | path read |\n|---|---|---|\n| ordinary | `main.go` | `main.go` — ok |\n| colon in the **file path**, ordinary repo | `od:d/mod.py` | `od` — wrong |\n| colon in the **repo key** | `main.go` | `Go` — wrong |\n\n**No unusual directory name is needed.** An ordinary GitHub-keyed repo containing a file at `od:d/mod.py` is enough, and the `len(parts) \u003c 5` guard does not fire (that ID splits into six fields).\n\n### Two data-integrity consequences\n\n1. **Relations redacted for files that are not ignored.** With a `.brainignore` rule for the directory `od`, `od:d/mod.py` is correctly kept — the file record and its symbols survive, because those are filtered by their real `file_path`. But every relation touching them was dropped, because the endpoint parse read `\"od\"`. One-sided, silent loss of the relation graph for a file the user never asked to hide. The stock ignore set (`build`, `vendor`, `dist`, `target`, `node_modules`) reaches this with no `.brainignore` at all: a file at `build:tools/mod.go` keeps its symbols and loses its edges.\n\n2. **Relations kept for files that are ignored.** A file endpoint has three fields, so `len(parts) \u003c 5` returned `\"\"` for *every* file endpoint and skipped the check entirely. entire-graph resolves imports and co-change pairs to `\u003crepo-key\u003e:file:\u003cpath\u003e` endpoints for files it emits no record for, so the ignored-ID set cannot cover them and the endpoint ID is the only signal — a relation naming `secret/config.go` went into the brain verbatim.\n\nThe function's own comment encoded the false premise (\"the repo key, language, and kind segments never contain `:`, so the path is always the third field\").\n\n## The fix\n\nA relation record has no `file_path` to read the path off: entire-graph's `RelationRecord` carries only `from_id`/`to_id`. The path has to come out of the ID, so the parse is now anchored on the two fields that are known to be free of `\":\"`.\n\n- **The repo key.** The snapshot header carries it, and entire-graph builds the header field and every ID from the same value (`sc.key`), so the prefix match is exact. An endpoint carrying a different repo key names no file in this repository and now resolves to no path rather than a spurious one.\n- **The kind**, which is always a bare token — no `\"/\"`, no `\".\"`.\n\nA **file** endpoint is then exact by construction: everything after the `file:` marker is the path, colons included. A **symbol** endpoint cuts the language at the first colon and takes the first split whose kind field is a bare token — which keeps a colon in the qualified name from stealing the split (`route` is accepted before `/users/:id` can be read as a kind) while stepping over one in the path (`d/mod.py` is rejected as a kind, so `od:d/mod.py` stays whole).\n\nEscaping or sanitising the repo key was not the fix here: the path injects colons through the same joint, so the consumer stays broken regardless.\n\n## Tests\n\n`internal/cli/semantic_endpoint_path_test.go`:\n\n- `TestSemanticEndpointPathRecoversFieldsAroundColons` — 15 cases across every ID shape: colon in the path, in the repo key, in the qualified name, in the path *and* the qualified name, file endpoints with and without a colon, externals, a foreign repo key, an opaque endpoint.\n- `TestSemanticIndexKeepsRelationsForColonPathOutsideIgnoredDirectory` — end to end through `runSemanticIndex` with a real `.brainignore`: a rule for `od` must not redact `od:d/mod.py`.\n- `TestSemanticIndexDropsRelationsForIgnoredColonPath` — the same path, genuinely ignored, is redacted.\n- `TestSemanticIndexDropsRelationsToIgnoredFileEndpoints` — relations naming ignored files only through file endpoint IDs are dropped and their paths do not appear in the persisted snapshot.\n- `TestFilterSemanticSnapshotHonorsColonPathEndpoints` — the re-filter path shares the parse and reaches the same verdicts.\n\n`TestSemanticEndpointPath` is retired in place; the new table covers all six of its cases.\n\n**Mutation-verified.** Restoring the `strings.Split` body behind the new signature (so it still compiles) fails four of the five tests, reproducing the exact wrong values above — `\"od\"` for `od:d/mod.py`, `\"Go\"` for the colon-in-repo-key case, `\"\"` for every file endpoint.\n\n`go test ./internal/cli -run 'Semantic|Ignore|Relation' -count=1` green; `go vet ./...` and `gofmt` clean.\n\n## Known residual\n\nThe kind anchor cannot separate a path whose colon-delimited tail segment is itself a bare token — a file literally named `od:README` still resolves to `od`. Closing that would mean pinning entire-graph's kind enum inside Brain, trading a contrived case for a drift hazard on every kind the provider adds. Every path shape with a `/` or a `.` after the colon — which is every path with a directory or an extension — resolves exactly.","created_at":"2026-08-29T22:38:48Z","draft":false,"head":"fix/semantic-endpoint-path-colon","head_repo":"entireio/entire-brain","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/pull/190","number":190,"sha":"d70e41e6c56cc4780f59fcce310d4e556fe4af4f","title":"fix(semantic): read a relation endpoint's path out of the ID correctly","updated_at":"2026-08-29T22:38:54Z"}
{"base":"main","body":"\u003c!-- entire-trail-link-start --\u003e\nhttps://entire.io/gh/entireio/entire-brain/trails/110\n\u003c!-- entire-trail-link-end --\u003e\n\n## The defect\n\n`memoryAdminCommandFixture` handed every command an `Options.Now` closure that incremented a plain `int`:\n\n```go\ncalls := 0\nopts := Options{..., Now: func() time.Time {\n    calls++\n    return manifest.GeneratedAt.Add(time.Duration(calls) * time.Second)\n}}\n```\n\nCommands built from those Options reach `driveManualAbstractJob`, which starts a coordinator heartbeat over that same clock:\n\n```go\nstopHeartbeat := coordinator.startHeartbeat(5*time.Second, c…9312 tokens truncated… the reader now gets, and how honest it is\n\nStep 3 states, without softening: phase 1 is deterministic and free; phase 2\n**spends tokens** (25 sessions per pass, `--backfill-budget`, `--no-backfill`);\nphase 3 installs **a persistent launchd/systemd service, by default, with no\nprompt** — `RunAtLoad`/`KeepAlive` on darwin, `Restart=always` on linux, both\nloaded into the live session — removable with `entire brain setup\n--uninstall-daemon`. There is no confirmation prompt anywhere in `runSetup`, and\nthe README now says so rather than implying one.\n\n### Prerequisites and ordering — determined from the code, not assumed\n\nThe old README listed \"Entire already enabled in the repository\" and \"target\nagent hooks already installed\" as prerequisites. Neither is one.\n\n`setup` hard-fails on exactly **one** thing: the target must be a local path that\nis a git repository. It is checked before any side effect (`internal/cli/setup.go`\n— `setup requires a local repository path` and `not a git repository: %s`,\nboth ahead of workspace registration, state writes and the daemon install).\n\nEverything else degrades:\n\n- **`entire enable` is not required and `setup` will not do it.**\n  `setupHookState` in `internal/cli/setup_status.go` is explicit: *\"setup\n  deliberately does NOT install it: hook registration belongs to the Entire CLI's\n  repository enablement (`entire enable`) … Duplicating it here would produce two\n  hooks racing to distill the same session.\"* `reportSetupHook` emits an advisory\n  `progress.Skip` line, never an error.\n- **Captured sessions are not required.** A failed export is reported as a failed\n  `sessions` component and the run falls back to a deterministic seed baseline\n  (`internal/cli/refresh.go`).\n- **The graph provider, a service manager and an agent CLI** each degrade only\n  their own phase.\n- `runSetup` returns non-zero only when the instant phase could not run at all or\n  **every** component failed.\n\nSo the documented order is `entire enable` → `entire brain setup`, stated as a\n**quality** decision rather than a correctness one, with the explicit note that\nrunning `enable` afterwards works too.\n\n### Verified by following the new text literally\n\nFresh sandbox — `HOME`, all four `XDG_*_HOME`, all four `ENTIRE_PLUGIN_*_DIR`,\n`ENTIRE_BRAIN_DAEMON_DIR`, `ENTIRE_BRAIN_DAEMON_NO_REGISTER=1` — executing the\nREADME's steps in order, on this branch's HEAD, with `--no-backfill` so nothing\nis spent. **The doc's default (no `--no-backfill`) is the path that spends.**\n\n| # | command (as written in the README) | exit |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `git clone https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain.git` | **0** |\n| 2 | `entire-brain/scripts/install.sh` (cloned the provider, route 3) | **0** |\n| 3 | `entire graph version` | **0** |\n| 4 | `entire brain version` | **0** |\n| 5 | `entire brain doctor` | **0** |\n| 6 | `cd \u003crepo\u003e` \u0026\u0026 `entire brain setup --no-backfill` | **0** |\n| 7 | `entire brain status` | **0** |\n| 8 | `entire brain overview` | **0** |\n| 9 | `entire brain brief \"\u003ctask\u003e\"` | **0** |\n\n**The final summary is not all-green, and that is the finding the new step 3a\ndocuments.** On a brand-new scratch repository it reads:\n\n```\nBrain ready (degraded) in 850ms\n  instant  + sessions  + seed  + docs  x semantic  + patterns  + entities  + memory\n           x semantic index: repo key mismatch: semantic snapshot repo_key\n             \"local/myrepo\" does not match current repo \"local/myrepo-888de9b32f15\"\n             hint: this repo has no git remote; add one (git remote add origin ...)\n  daemon   - installed but not running (io.entire.brain-watch.a2d3fd66)\n```\n\nA second sandbox repository, identical but **with** a git remote, builds\n`semantic` fine and fails `sessions` instead\n(`checkpoint_scope_incomplete: Entire returned no readable checkpoint IDs`) —\nisolating the two causes cleanly. Both exit 0. Step 3a now names both, with the\nfix for each (`git remote add origin …`; `entire enable`).\n\nThe one deliberate sandbox exemption, stated rather than hidden: the `git clone`\nof the private `entireio/entire-brain` ran with the real `HOME`, because git's\ncredential helper is `gh auth git-credential`, gh's token lives in the macOS\nlogin keychain, and `security` locates that keychain through `$HOME`. A plain\n`git clone` touches no Entire state, so this weakens nothing the sandbox exists\nto protect. Every command after it was fully sandboxed.\n\n**Real registry untouched**, checked against a snapshot taken before the run:\n\n- `~/.local/share/entire` and `~/.config/entire` — byte-identical listings.\n- `~/Library/LaunchAgents` — identical.\n- `launchctl list` — **zero** labels matching `entire` or `brain`; the only diff\n  is macOS `com.apple.mdworker.shared.*` churn.\n- The plist that *would* have been installed was written inside the sandbox:\n  `.../sandbox/daemon/io.entire.brain-watch.a2d3fd66.plist`.\n\n`sh -n` and `shellcheck` clean on `scripts/install.sh`; the new banner was\nrendered through `sh`'s heredoc to confirm the escaped backticks come out right.\n","created_at":"2026-08-19T18:49:40Z","draft":false,"head":"feat/brain-setup-onboarding","head_repo":"entireio/entire-brain","html_url":"https://github.com/entireio/entire-brain/pull/89","number":89,"sha":"f769e9b1d3fa74174dd3f075a054585cdcafc822","title":"One-command onboarding: entire-brain setup (instant core, capped backfill, watch daemon)","updated_at":"2026-09-06T04:01:16Z"}
